Nanotechnology has been promoting the development of ultrasensitive detection of biomarkers on diverse platforms including chromatic, electrochemical, chemiluminescent, and fluorescent assays for decades1–5. The realization of rapid onsite point-of-care bioassays is highly desired particularly for applications in the agriculture, environmental monitoring, and disease diagnosis6,7. The successful detection of a biomarker relies on the efficiency of both the bio-recognition of the analyte and the signal transducing. Among various bioassay platforms, chromatic (optical) assays embrace the features of simple operation and rapid response. However, they usually lack high sensitivity due to the limited signal transducing efficiency per bio-recognizing event8. Thus, tremendous efforts have been directed towards amplified signal transduction employing nanomaterials8–12.
Immunoassay-based technologies used for the detection of biomarkers have been widely studied due to the outstanding inherent specificity of immunoreactions13–18. An ultrasensitive immunoassay requires a well-designed label for signal indication and amplification to realize efficient detection of the labeled antigen-antibody complex in the signal transducing procedure. Typical labels including natural enzymes, gold nanoparticles, quantum dots, carbon nanotubes, and mesoporous nanostructures widely reported in recent years have provided flexible choices for the construction of immunosensors19,20. However, the limitation of relatively low detection accuracy, which is mainly due to limited biorecognizing event, inferior catalytic activity, and poor water solubility21 of nanomaterial-based artificial enzymes, remain as a main challenge restricting their applications22.
Nanozymes are considered as promising substitutes of natural enzymes for ELISA, among which MOFs have been reported as high-efficiency and economical nanozymes due to their outstanding surface area and abundant active sites23–27. However, a major issue is to improve their poor water solubility and inferior catalytic activity compared to HRP2. As for the applications of nanozymes in biological assays, good water solubility or dispersibility is significant because most of the analytical reactions occur in aqueous conditions. Thus, good solubility or dispersibility is necessary to guarantee the homogenous analytical and reaction environments and the reproductivity. Instead of improving the catalytic capability of a unit of nanozyme, ingenious approaches to encapsulate multiple signal units in one label per biorecognition event can strikingly amplify the assay sensitivity28.
A variety of biomarkers of woodsmoke exposure, including BPDE-DNA, originate from the intake of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAHs). PAHs are a family of chemicals derived from the incomplete combustion of organic materials; they are common environmental pollutants existing in vehicle exhaust, tobacco smoke, grilled foods, and polluted air29. After the inhalation of benzo[a]pyrene, a kind of PAHs, human metabolism converts it into benzo[a]pyrene-7,8-diol-9,10- epoxide (BPDE), which can form DNA adducts (BPDE-DNA) through covalently binding with guanine and adenine residues. The unrepaired adducts can  cause permanent mutations and eventually tumors or cancers30. Thus, ultrasensitive analysis of BPDE-DNA is significant for the assessment and prevention of cancer risk at an early stage. Approaches to quantify the level of BPDE-DNA in blood include high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) and gas/liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC/LC-MS), both of which are extremely costly, time-consuming, and unreachable for onsite/household assessment31,32. Immunoassays, including ELISA, lateral flow immunoassays, and nanomaterial-based immunoassays, offer rapid and convenient alternatives to chromatography for BPDE-DNA detection32–34. A validation of an immunoassaybased capillary electrophoresis-laser induced fluorescence (CE-LIF) method to analyze the concentration of BPDE-DNA in blood was reported by LeBlanc et al. in $2009^{35}$ . Wang and co-workers designed an immunoassay employing fluorescence imaging to count the amount of OliGreen dye specifically adsorbed to ssDNA for the quantification of BPDE-DNA36. A similar work was conducted by Wang’s group using a quantum dots (QDs) enhanced immunoassay coupled with a CE-LIF detector37. However, these methods either require complicated analyzers and equipment to realize ultrasensitive analysis or are challenged with the purification of unbound labels.
In this work, we investigated the approach to fabricate a nanozyme enriched 2D GO/Fe-MOF structure, namely Nanozyme Nest, with superior catalytic ability. The structure and peroxidase-like catalytic activity of the Nanozyme Nest were characterized, optimized and compared with HRP and Fe-MOF. The synthesized Nanozyme Nest was then employed in the ultrasensitive ELISA for the determination of BPDEDNA (Scheme 1). The present study embraces the following main advantages: 1) we designed a novel structure of nanozymes with dual-amplified catalytic ability (from GO and Fe-MOF); 2) the approach overcame the inherently poor water solubility of Fe-MOF; 3) the obtained Nanozyme Nest provided a low-cost, stable, and efficient alternative to natural enzymes for ELISA with high sensitivity comparable to HPLC31 and GC/LC-MS38 methods.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Instruments. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was carried out on a FEI Technai G2 20 Twin TEM equipped with a $200~\mathrm{KV}$ LaB6 electron source. ELISA was carried out using a Tecan SafireII multi-mode plate reader (Tecan Trading AG, Switzerland). The Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy was recorded with a NICOLET iS50 FTIR spectrophotometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA). The specific surface area of the samples was investigated with an automatic volumetric sorption analyzer (ASAP 2020, Micromeritics Instrument Corp., Norcross, GA). Zeta potential analysis was conducted on a Zetasizer Nano ZS (Malvern Instruments Inc., Westborough, MA). Eppendorf 5417C centrifuge (Eppendorf North America, Hauppauge, NY) and Crest ultrasonic cleaner (WF-120UH, Crest Ultrasonics Corp., Ewing Township, NJ) were used in the study.
Materials and Reagents. 2-Aminoterephthalic acid $(99\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), iron(III) chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(FeCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , $97\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), acetic acid $(\geq99.7\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), succinic anhydride $(\geq99\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), potassium carbonate $\mathrm{{K}}_{2}\mathrm{{CO}}_{3}$ , $299.995\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), $^{3,3^{\circ},5,5^{\circ}-}$ tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) liquid peroxidase substrate (Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), phosphate buffered saline (PBS, Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), bovine serum albumin (BSA, Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), sucrose $(\geq99.5\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), succinic anhydride $(\geq99\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), graphite flakes $99\%$ carbon basis, 325 mesh particle size, Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), sodium nitrate $(\geq99.0\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), sulfuric acid $(99.999\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), potassium permanganate (Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), hydrogen peroxide solution $30\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), hydrochloric acid (HCl, $37\%$ , Millipore Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), TWEEN-20 (Millipore
Sigma Co., St. Louis, MO), BPDE-DNA standards (Cell Biolabs Inc., San Diego, CA), BPDE-DNA antibody (AntiBPDE-Ab-I, $200~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ , Santa Cruz Biotechnology Inc., Dallas, TX), goat anti-mouse IgG $(\mathrm{H}\mathrm{+}\mathrm{L})$ secondary antibody (Ab-II, Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA).
Synthesis of Fe-MOF. Fe-MOF $\mathrm{(Fe-MIL-88NH_{2})}$ ) was prepared through a facile method based on previous literature39,40. Firstly, $_{0.027\mathrm{~g~}}$ of 2-aminoterephthalic acid, $_{0.041\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $6.215~\mathrm{\mg}$ of acetic acid were mixed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The mixture was then heated in a hydrothermal reactor at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{~h~}$ . The product was then centrifuged at $6000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ and washed with ethanol several times. Finally, the Fe-MOF was dried in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and stored at room temperature.
Fabrication of GO/Fe-MOF. Graphene oxide was prepared by the Hummers’ method41. The obtained GO was then mixed with the Fe-MOF prepared above, followed by ultrasound treatment for $10~\mathrm{\min}$ . The GO/Fe-MOF was constructed via the physical mix of GO and Fe-MOF.
Preparation of GO/Fe-MOF-Secondary Antibody Conjugate. The secondary antibody (Ab-II) was conjugated with the GO/Fe-MOF Nanozyme Nest according to the literature42. Firstly, $50~\mathrm{mM}$ of ${\bf K}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ was used to adjust the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the GO/Fe-MOF solution to $8.3{\pm}0.1\$ , and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Ab-II $\mathrm{(1~mg/mL)}$ ) was then added to $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of the prepared solution and incubated for $8\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the GO/Fe-MOF-Ab-II was washed three times using PBS and dispersed in PBS containing $2\ \mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ of BSA and $3\ \mathrm{\wt\%}$ of sucrose. The concentration of the GO/Fe-MOF-Ab-II was $10\mathrm{mg/mL}$ .
Immunoassay to Detect the BPDE-DNA adduct. The procedures to determine the level of BPDE-DNA in human blood through the ultrasensitive ELISA method employing the Nanozyme Nest was shown in Scheme 1. The BPDE-DNA analysis was carried out by three main steps as follows: firstly (a-b), the BPDE-DNA from DNA extraction was immobilized on the 96-well plate. Basically, (a) $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of DNA sample or BPDE-DNA standard was added into the wells of a DNA binding plate and incubated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ with gentle shaking. (b) The wells were washed with PBST buffer (PBS containing $0.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of TWEEN-20) for 3 times, and then 200 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of PBST containing $1\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ BSA was added into each well for blocking at $37~^{\mathrm{~0}}\mathrm{C}$ for $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ ; secondly (c), the BPDE antibody was specifically bonded to the immobilized BPDEDNA. Simply, the wells were washed 3 times with PBST buffer, and $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 1000-fold-diluted anti-BPDE-I antibody was added to each well and incubated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ ; thirdly (d-e), the secondary antibody with conjugated Nanozyme Nest was bonded to the BPDE antibody followed by a chromogenic reaction. In detail, (d) all the wells were washed 5 times using PBST, and $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 500-fold-diluted GO/Fe-MOF-Ab-II conjugate $20~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ was added to each well. The wells were incubated at $37~^{\mathrm{{0}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . (e) The plate was washed 5 times, and $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of TMB substrate solution was added to each well (including the blank control well). The mixture was incubated for $10~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature on an orbital shaker, and 50 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ HCl was added to each well to stop the reaction. The chromatic signals (the absorbance at $450~\mathrm{nm}$ ) indicating the concentrations of the target were then captured by the ELISA plate reader.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Design and Characterization of the Nanozyme Nest. As shown in Scheme 2, the $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ generation labels used for ELISA are natural enzymes, like peroxidase, oxidase, and superoxide dismutase, which have significant applications in medicine, food safety, and disease diagnosis. However, their drawbacks, particularly the low stability and environmental dependent performance, restrict their practical applications8. Nanozymes as the $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ generation labels provide promising options to design bioassays with good stability, easy purification, and relatively low cost, but the catalytic ability of nanozymes is inferior compared to natural enzymes, which results in challenges for ultrasensitive analysis. Furthermore, the poor solubility of nanomaterial-based artificial enzymes remains as a large issue.
Thus, a $3^{\mathrm{rd}}$ generation nanozyme label providing amplified catalytic ability and enhanced water solubility is demanded. We proposed a bio-amplification strategy to design the GO/Fe-MOF Nanozyme Nest as a promising option. Particles of Fe-MOF were first synthesized and then assembled with GO as substituent catalytic units for HRP with peroxidase-like activity39. Figure 1A shows the TEM image of the octahedral Fe-MOF nanocrystals with an average diameter of approximately $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The size of the Fe-MOF crystals has significant influence on their catalytic activity. The crystal size can be controlled by adjusting the molar ratio of acetic acid/ferric chloride, of which a higher ratio results in a larger crystal size. The higher acetic acid/ferric chloride ratio also provides a better-defined crystal morphology, because the acetic acid species control the nucleation, the crystal growth and the deprotonation degree of the linker molecules40. The processes of synthesis are illustrated in Figure 1. The TEM images of the GO/Fe- MOF Nanozyme Nest are shown in Figure 1C & D. The images of the Fe-MOF (Figure 1A) and the graphene oxide (Figure 1B) are also included for comparison. The graphene oxide flakes acted like sheets and the octahedral Fe-MOF crystals filled between the GO layers in a uniform and well-defined manner. The positively charged Fe-MOF particles were tightly adsorbed onto the surface of the negatively charged GO nanosheets due to the electrostatic interaction between them, resulting in the formation of a GO/Fe-MOF structure. The successful conjugation was validated by zeta potential analysis shown in the supporting information (Figure S1). The zeta potential of GO increased from $-57.6~\mathrm{mV}$ to $-43.2~\mathrm{mV}$ after conjugation with Fe-MOF. The GO/Fe-MOF was then functionalized with Ab-II to form the conjugate by the passive absorption through electrostatic interactions. Simply by adjusting the $\mathfrak{p H}$ to $8.3{\pm}0.1$ , slightly higher than the isoelectric point of the antibody, the negatively charged GO/Fe-MOF can tightly bind to the positively charged groups of the antibody. The successful fabrication of the GO/Fe-MOF-Ab-II was validated by zeta potential analysis presented in Figure S1. With the modification of the positively charged antibody (Ab-II) onto the negatively charged GO/Fe-MOF, the corresponding zeta potential increases from $-43.2~\mathrm{mV}$ to $-28.4~\mathrm{mV}$ . The Nanozyme Nest incorporating dual peroxidase-like catalytic ability from the Fe-MOF and $\mathrm{GO}^{43}$ nanozymes, along with the enrichment effect of Fe-MOF and the improved water solubility from GO, eventually resulted in an efficient prelabeled GO/Fe-MOF-AbII conjugate for use in an ultrasensitive sandwich-type ELISA. The Fourier transform infrared spectra of the GO/Fe-MOFAntibody and GO/Fe-MOF were compared in Figure S2. The sharp peak located at $1650~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in GO/Fe-MOF-Antibody corresponds to the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{N}=\mathbf{C}$ vibration in the antibody, demonstrating that the antibody has been successfully conjugated with $_\mathrm{GO/Fe-MOF^{44}}$ .
Catalytic Activity and Water Solubility of the Nanozyme Nest. The peroxidase-like activity of the Nanozyme Nest was validated by the catalytic oxidation of TMB in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . As shown in Figure 2, the blue color originates from the oxidation of TMB. The catalytic activity of the Nanozyme Nest is due to the existence of both the iron ions and the GO. Basically, the nanozyme can induce the generation of hydroxyl radicals (·OH) through the Fenton reaction45 between iron and hydrogen peroxide, and then the ·OH oxidizes the TMB substrate to produce a diimine, which causes the solution to become blue. The GO contributes crucially for the catalytic activity by: 1) enhancing the diffusion of the ions and free radicals; 2) working as an electron donor for the π-cation interaction between GO and $\mathrm{Fe-MOF^{43}}$ . Compared to HRP and noble metal nanozymes46, the transition metal nanozyme Fe-MOF has both high stability at relatively harsh conditions, like temperature and $\mathfrak{p H}$ , and economical cost39. These factors that affect the catalytic reaction of the GO/Fe-MOF were also examined. We investigated the catalytic activity of the GO/FeMOF from $-20$ to $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S3). As the temperature changed from ${}^{-20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}$ to $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the catalytic activity of the GO/Fe-MOF was retained more than $50\%$ . We further challenged the catalytic reaction with the pH ranging from 3.5 to 9.5 (Figure S3). The proposed GO/Fe-MOF exhibited optimized catalytic activity at a $\mathfrak{p H}$ value of 3.5 and presented more than $50\%$ catalytic activity in a wide $\mathfrak{p H}$ range from 3.5 to 7.5. These results indicated that the GO/Fe-MOF possessed high stability at relatively harsh conditions. The peroxidaselike activity of Fe-MOF was demonstrated to be even higher than HRP at high concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and exhibits higher affinity towards $\mathrm{TMB}^{39}$ . Furthermore, the high surface area of the Fe-MOF nanozyme enhanced its peroxidase-mimicking efficiency due to more active cites accessible to reaction agents. Specific surface areas of the as-prepared Fe-MOF and GO/Fe-MOF were confirmed by nitrogen sorption-desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure S4). Both the curves can be identified as type-IV isotherms, with a pronounced hysteresis loop at the middle pressure region, suggesting the existence of a mesoporous structure. And the expressive hysteresis characteristic at low relative pressure regions corresponds to the numerous micropores.  It can be calculated that both the materials have large Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of 445.78 and $798.034~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . GO plays an important role for the higher surface area of the GO/Fe-MOF, leading to a high peroxidase-like activity.
The ratio of GO to Fe-MOF is crucial for the efficacy of the immunoassay. The influence of the GO:Fe-MOF ratio can be observed by measuring the peroxidase-like activity of the GO:Fe-MOF (Figure 2): by adding the $\mathrm{TMB}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ substrate, a blue color was produced from the formation of the diimine generated through the oxidation of TMB in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ; when keeping reducing the ratio (increasing Fe-MOF concentration), the blue color became deeper, and some precipitates were even formed at a high concentration of FeMOF. The color change was due to the increased enrichment degree of Fe-MOF particles per unit GO sheet, while the precipitation could be the result of the low water solubility of $\mathrm{MOF^{47-49}}$ . Thus, the optimal GO:Fe-MOF ratio was determined to be 1:5. Poor water solubility of nanozymes is known as a shortcoming for biorthogonal catalysis46. In this work, the proposed Nanozyme Nest shows better water solubility21 compared to Fe-MOF due to the modification via $\mathrm{GO}^{50}$ .
The catalytic properties of nanozymes are dependent on their kinetic constants51. We evaluated the Michaelis-Menten kinetics of HRP, Fe-MOF, and GO/Fe-MOF according to standard protocols52,53. The Michaelis constants $(K_{\mathrm{m}})$ were calculated by nonlinearly fitting the Michaelis-Menten curves shown in Figure S5 and summarized in Table 1. Smaller $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ values indicate stronger affinity between the enzyme and the substrate. The $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ of the synthesized GO/Fe-MOF with TMB as the substrate is lower than that of Fe-MOF and HRP, suggesting that the proposed Nanozyme Nest has higher affinity for TMB. The possible reason may be that the GO/FeMOF Nanozyme Nest has more active sites than Fe-MOF and HRP.
Detection of the Woodsmoke Biomarker (BPDE-DNA). The performance of the proposed GO/Fe-MOF Nanozyme Nest was characterized by detecting the DNA adduct of BPDE. The detection was realized via the conventional sandwich enzyme-linked immunoassay. The Ab-I bound to the incubated BPDE-DNA sample, and then the GO/Fe-MOF-AbII conjugate, acting as an indicator of the BPDE-DNA level, bound to the Ab-I. As shown in Figure 3, the absorbance (at $652\mathrm{nm}$ ) of the blue color measured by the ELISA plate reader increased linearly with the concentration of the BPDE-DNA analyte. The limit of detection (LOD) was calculated to be $1.729\mathrm{\ng/mL}$ and $0.268\mathrm{\textrm{~}}\mathrm{ng/mL}$ based on $3\times$ standard deviation/slope by using Fe-MOF and GO/Fe-MOF, respectively. The superior sensitivity may be attributed to the higher enzyme-like activity of GO/Fe-MOF than Fe-MOF (Figure 4) and the better water solubility of GO/Fe-MOF (Figure S6). Thus, the peroxidase-like activity and feasibility of amplification through the GO/Fe-MOF Nanozyme Nest were validated. As shown in Table 2, compared to previously reported analytical techniques including liquid chromatography and the immunoassay-based methods, the proposed GO/Fe-MOF Nanozyme Nest employed in the ultrasensitive ELISA exhibits superior detection performance. In addition, we employed the proposed assay to measure human serum samples with spiked BPDE-DNA. The detection results were validated by a commercial ELISA kit, and the obtained recovery was in the range of $94.20\substack{-121.36\%}$ (Table S1). These results demonstrate that the ultrasensitive ELISA holds great promise for BPDE-DNA detection with a great accuracy and reliability.
## CONCLUSIONS
We have successfully synthesized a GO/Fe-MOF Nanozyme Nest and demonstrated its robust peroxidase-like catalytic activity. The ultrasensitive ELISA method based on the Nanozyme Nest was developed for the quantification of a woodsmoke biomarker (BPDE-DNA adduct). The superior detection sensitivity is due to the significantly amplified peroxidase-like catalytic activity of the enriched Fe-MOF nanozyme and the cooperated GO. The integrated Nanozyme Nest provides enhanced sensitivity through dual-amplified catalytic activity and improved water solubility, in addition to the characteristics of classic nanozymes including excellent stability, low cost, and easy purification. The Nanozyme Nest represents a compelling new strategy for bio-amplification using nanozymes for immunoassays on various platforms. While the Nanozyme Nest based ultrasensitive ELISA requires the use of large analytical equipment like the microplate reader, which prevents its applications for household or onsite rapid diagnosis, it can be solved by developing a portable optical device for simplified readout. The ultrasensitive ELISA could be further combined with a smartphone-based portable reader54–57 to enable the onsite detection. Furthermore, the amplification strategy for developing ultrasensitive immunosensors and biochemical analysis demonstrates the great potential for the applications of the nanozymes in disease diagnosis, food safety, and environmental monitoring.
